  "body": "### Problem\n\nClicking **View routing / 查看路由** in an expanded historical Usage entry changes the view to Routing, but does not bring the selected request's trace into the visible viewport. To the user it looks as though the link only opens the Routing page and displays no matching result.\n\nObserved on the official Docker image **0.1.1472**, in a real browser with historical routing data.\n\n### Reproduction\n\n1. Have persisted historical requests and a scrollable Routing view.\n2. Open Usage and expand a failed model-group request (the example here has three attempts).\n3. Click **View routing / 查看路由**.\n4. Observe that the page switches to `?view=routing`, but the corresponding trace/details are not visible.\n\n### What was verified\n\nThis is not a missing trace or an unimplemented route endpoint in the reproduced case:\n\n- Fetching `/api/gateway/route?id=<route-id>&day=2026-10-25` returned HTTP 200 with the correct route ID and all three attempts.\n- After actually clicking the Usage button, the Routing DOM contained the selected request's specific unsupported-parameter error.\n- However, the Routing container's `scrollTop` was approximately **4659 px**, and the matching detail's bounding rectangle had **top ≈ -2352 px** in a **1215 px** high viewport. The selected result was above the visible area.\n\nThus the navigation selected/loaded the record, but left it off-screen. Exact scroll positions will depend on prior state and viewport size.\n\n### Expected behavior\n\nNavigating from Usage should bring the target historical routing record and/or its details into view and visibly identify the selected request. Preserve the correct date/record selection. If a trace is unavailable, show an explicit missing/expired-trace message instead of an apparently unrelated Routing page.\n\n### Relevant code\n\nThe Usage button calls `window.openRoute(r.route_id, r.t)`. The Routing `openRoute` path fetches the record, changes the view, and calls `pick(r)`. The latter has logic/comments intended to preserve page position while selecting entries.\n\nPreserving position may be useful for selection within Routing, but the observed cross-view navigation needs to reveal the selected destination. This is a source-level lead, not a claim that a particular scrolling fix has already been tested.\n\n### Related\n\n#3623 covers the separate Usage presentation problem: aggregate attempts are shown alongside only the final provider/error, hiding the preceding failover chain. Fixing this navigation would make the existing detailed trace usable, while that issue asks for clear attempt history and labels in Usage itself.\n\nNo raw request exports are attached because they contain private conversation and tool content.",
